// Single-objective robust anchors for the two Pareto corner points, computed by
// the (independent) robust single-objective code path.
//
// Minimal cost is hand-derivable. Writing V, V1, V2 for the worst-case expected
// cost from states 0, 1, 2 (state 3 onwards is free), and noting that nature
// maximises cost so puts its 0.75 upper bound on the costlier successor:
//   V1 = 1.5 + 0.75V, V2 = 2.5 + 0.75V, and V2 > V1, so
//   V  = 1 + 0.75*V2 + 0.25*V1 = 3.25 + 0.75V, giving V = 13.
// RESULT: 13
R{"cost"}minmax=? [ C ];

// RESULT: 80/13
R{"gain"}maxmin=? [ C ];

// The same two values via the multi-objective solver, one objective at a time.
// RESULT: 13
multi(R{"cost"}min=? [ C ], R{"gain"}>=0 [ C ]);

// RESULT: 80/13
multi(R{"gain"}max=? [ C ], R{"cost"}<=1000 [ C ]);

// The Pareto curve itself: this is the query that used to fail outright with
// "Iterative method did not converge within 10000 iterations". Its corner
// points are the two single-objective values above — (13, 0) never gathers, and
// 80/13 is the maximum robustly guaranteed gain; the other point is
// (461/26, 80/13). Pareto points are compared with an absolute tolerance of
// 1e-5 and must be written as decimals, hence the tightened -epsilon in .args.
// RESULT: [(13,0),(17.73076923,6.15384615)]
multi(R{"cost"}min=? [ C ], R{"gain"}max=? [ C ]);
